    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f3bf105d-12ab-5f1d-9edc-100bc3b78217",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 13 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{12}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A &=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}-2C_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}+3C_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 13 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{1}:=C_{1}-2C_{2}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}+3C_{2}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
